#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "1abed37b-99f8-5298-bc7b-f80259c78398" :question/math-academy-id "e-7446" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/1a/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2pt \\\\ -2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "af2757f8-0392-54c8-b681-162ee13b1804" :question/math-academy-id "e-7447" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/83/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "780170af-a92e-5a87-b08d-d9d940d6906b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7448" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ae9f63-6af5-5a84-a916-c7136c71812f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238093} {:answer/id #uuid "8f37eba5-4cd0-5732-ae8e-d1ad135f3c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239303} {:answer/id #uuid "d12f983a-1b18-54f5-8284-f89b521e5668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239888} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b4c884-481d-5883-899d-991540722a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239947} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cbf3ff-7697-5769-9290-35fdcd784b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9cbf3ff-7697-5769-9290-35fdcd784b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240112} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b4838b-8cd7-57f0-a4f3-f65d787298e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9977732a-3153-5e0c-bb69-50c046885f9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106447" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d828ed6-02a5-556a-bcf0-6de2c7929742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-t,\\,\\,y+2=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548411} {:answer/id #uuid "9e778123-9ee0-54cc-9eb6-f27d0cb7ddbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548666} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4dd6aa-918f-5eba-ab69-561c4e51cffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2t,\\,\\,y-2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548691} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548737} {:answer/id #uuid "cbac0851-5569-524e-ac10-92470ba43d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548737} :answer-field/id #uuid "829f2828-1700-5400-9439-0d028651d780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e648a6d-37fa-5da0-b09f-42f9d00baf66" :question/math-academy-id "q-106451" :question/problem "![](images/96/96e1647f6069dee4219f8cb92e3fb4277396394c016b27ecd4de557f87257000.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(3,-2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-6),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(-1)t,\\,y+2=(-1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2c542b-14eb-50f7-91cd-7cf55b63715c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238308} {:answer/id #uuid "23d60b13-a420-5f8b-8f7c-72a3ee1d397a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238369} {:answer/id #uuid "282c0c3b-562f-5d91-b69e-803ddc2db755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238406} {:answer/id #uuid "c2295498-cda9-5ad7-b191-67bb906c9da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239759} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d6753f-5832-55e2-a745-782efccc7576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2295498-cda9-5ad7-b191-67bb906c9da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239759} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5773ff-e00d-500c-9e70-5df8626667fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36c7a66b-6181-5e37-8eb4-d8f285049a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-106455" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa76345-e0ea-5f04-b7ea-1791d5e69dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238187} {:answer/id #uuid "41461211-ade1-50fb-b1ff-922e20b44a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238624} {:answer/id #uuid "49befa63-d348-54fa-a145-bb725fd44d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238696} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dd3860-ad4a-5a00-b345-413e1a5e5d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240206} {:answer/id #uuid "f80863b3-de5f-5b13-bd06-a70bbf012a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa76345-e0ea-5f04-b7ea-1791d5e69dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c979fc5-9af9-56b4-a0fc-1e477194f87d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53bdcf57-c309-5f31-a918-fde0b0925ad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-106461" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e4ca77-f2af-5110-bdff-f4762cbcd8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241214} {:answer/id #uuid "064d8cce-843b-5ad0-87ca-f878c2e21278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241236} {:answer/id #uuid "25bad7a1-76dd-58ae-9f5b-e2231867653d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241503} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef6ba9e-c87d-52c1-9732-615a663d486a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241743} {:answer/id #uuid "8b335e25-8957-510c-896e-d1259089977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef6ba9e-c87d-52c1-9732-615a663d486a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241743} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63647b7-705a-53df-a012-13dc9c87b299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc14080e-5154-5a78-ad65-c0fdceff6c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106502" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e072a8a-4c4e-573b-8869-941c8567a11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=8(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548445} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a134ec0-3a05-56eb-ad2c-0e83ebd565ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548582} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5d3114-e499-590c-baf7-861605384f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548587} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78eb36-9d33-50e7-9b6c-c918281f4ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548562} :answer-field/id #uuid "314ab3a0-11b2-5836-a9a6-779f4e2cfc86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-106506" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y+3=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+1}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{2}) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300393} :answer-field/id #uuid "4339e742-90db-5fe5-8713-242c87bd162a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17ed819-e5df-5f2a-85be-61fbd0c56120" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df968d14-24c9-53a0-ac49-2683ed9a8cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-321592" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441df4d0-5131-54a1-933b-f1384c81ed4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352194} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf4916d-f01a-5c33-ab05-5d7802093fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352648} {:answer/id #uuid "a9425634-d635-528c-9bd8-3e80c442d03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353002} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a39da-13cd-5859-89cd-ac0c7adad417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353649} {:answer/id #uuid "f1435b12-8e14-5660-ae0a-19a587cb9590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bf4916d-f01a-5c33-ab05-5d7802093fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352648} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c550a8-9f9a-5976-b63a-664e3b368ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c9ce835-d453-5565-8c95-6082447bc008" :question/math-academy-id "q-84819" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2pt \\\\ 4 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548492} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd94bab-ba3d-53e6-b7a3-be9c9c7e8fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y-3=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548532} {:answer/id #uuid "606af933-7d01-5aba-a01d-1698e07eb2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{8}{5}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548536} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7ca21-f69d-5100-a76d-a62b09fb4809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{8}{5}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548663} {:answer/id #uuid "e06459cd-3712-586f-bc68-69909dacd4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{5}{4}t,\\,y-3=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548492} :answer-field/id #uuid "46647a5c-2b95-53fe-940a-e6b35e9ddd8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ba3cd0d-7f42-50d0-93f6-108a716173e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84826" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,3),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5=2pt \\\\ 5=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =p{(\\frac{5}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{25}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=2(\\frac{5}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b17b005-eeb9-5b45-8c4e-b6cf57ce3220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351811} {:answer/id #uuid "352cb32e-5d32-597a-ac9d-bd2257ed30f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352063} {:answer/id #uuid "9a47665d-f326-53b6-b698-e7af9a118d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352891} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a8bade-5e49-5176-9d09-2af5dff62a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353077} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f75101-6991-5c20-a1c1-53dbc6c87785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b17b005-eeb9-5b45-8c4e-b6cf57ce3220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351811} :answer-field/id #uuid "f59570fd-6d43-585f-9b41-1fb122f41d08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d45f6ac-8de5-558d-a5e4-8428086d3905" :question/math-academy-id "q-84840" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003023ae-1aa2-54a4-98df-d8e7ff22066d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351563} {:answer/id #uuid "04a61724-2b44-5a05-b9c5-ee7e18a029b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+3=12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351612} {:answer/id #uuid "249bff29-1e23-57f0-a40d-6219e1c53f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351902} {:answer/id #uuid "252094cb-c77d-5625-a2e6-f8d7aa811973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351906} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dbece5-e2b7-5256-a030-b0f252658ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "252094cb-c77d-5625-a2e6-f8d7aa811973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351906} :answer-field/id #uuid "8926ebe9-9279-5214-b8aa-a91752679929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1303c715-2341-5c20-852f-40641cdc1a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-84844" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ebd37e-c666-5413-8dba-6706d27b3ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486695} {:answer/id #uuid "5471d6a5-d5f1-5e64-b0d4-d247884f4266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486776} {:answer/id #uuid "68408a08-0e06-57f9-927a-8ec79117001a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486821} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a770e-5b76-5688-9d7d-d367b29628f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486837} {:answer/id #uuid "956916df-320e-5b5e-b828-825e40cf6ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{8}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a770e-5b76-5688-9d7d-d367b29628f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486837} :answer-field/id #uuid "d889d998-6d7a-58b2-a490-fd41b8228c67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da2c9d27-2db1-52e6-a2e3-b28e1798b755" :question/math-academy-id "q-84846" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-5,-2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p(x+5) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y+2=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d15c961-ce4c-5b0f-b198-b69c1a414f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2={t}^{2},\\,y-6=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352270} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5e08ab-abba-5627-a6a3-ed17cb3327d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=4{t}^{2},\\,y+6=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352773} {:answer/id #uuid "b234bffb-b6a4-5128-832e-fe2b466e9c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353089} {:answer/id #uuid "bef09309-7f97-5138-bfd2-1e56684eda70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353203} {:answer/id #uuid "c344a5c7-5e96-5669-9478-22aa0d265417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-6=8{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef09309-7f97-5138-bfd2-1e56684eda70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353203} :answer-field/id #uuid "28815b6d-4153-5762-8444-6997485e6fec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f197f0f5-2e24-5c55-8528-25906de0f7eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-84847" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "761e2e34-2b73-575e-ad93-69b3ef7a4509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=8(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548573} {:answer/id #uuid "81396d4d-0bd7-575b-b2e7-739740b6660c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548600} {:answer/id #uuid "830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548606} {:answer/id #uuid "bce99d0a-b922-5732-8a5d-54c719314068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548731} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fc5336-dfe4-5a79-95c6-2401457bbad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548606} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae7850-0329-5a60-9d70-6fd1640a128d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "727c8803-de94-5b7f-a91c-cc70ef9900c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84849" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =2(x-4),\\,t=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=2(x-4)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288c1c06-2498-55be-af84-64025de5b036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351943} {:answer/id #uuid "4759ba61-c36a-5eeb-bf3c-db6d3982dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352227} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9c51cd-97fe-5ec1-81c9-933a63d4893c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352260} {:answer/id #uuid "5e61dff6-cf64-5212-9dbd-51badf87ecb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-5(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352402} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5df374-7f86-5a06-97b0-4c7c7a354539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5df374-7f86-5a06-97b0-4c7c7a354539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353169} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cba858-60e5-5d69-a896-3a09d3f84f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f7f6549-2721-5283-9cbb-bd6bbc1d1262" :question/math-academy-id "q-84859" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
